    kg:textSpan "Design Compiler (DC). We get another key observation that if we keep full precision for the intermediate results after multiplication, 8-bit oatingpoint multiplier consumes less area than 8-bit xed-point multiplier. For instance, 8-bit oating-point multiplier with M 4 E 3 consumes only 12.3% area of the 8-bit xed-point multiplier. This is because M 4 E 3 only needs a 5-bit unsigned xed-point multiplier and a 3-bit unsigned xed-point adder. The area savings are more signicant for 8-bit oating-poi" .

<http://example.org/kg/chunk/2003_03603_rag__chunk_001> a kg:Chunk ;
    kg:chunkId "2003.03603_rag::chunk_001" ;
    kg:docId "2003.03603_rag" ;
    kg:inPaper <http://example.org/kg/paper/2003_03603_rag> ;
    kg:textSpan "is an eective way to improve the execution latency and energy eciency. Existing quantization methods [ 50 , 10 , 52 , 49 , 24 ] generally require training data for calibration or ne-tuning. Nevertheless, in many real-world applications in medical [ 47 ], nance [ 48 ] and industrial domains, the training data may not be available due to privacy or condentiality issues. Consequently, these methods are no longer applicable due to the absence of training data. Therefore, the data-free quantization i" .

<http://example.org/kg/chunk/2003_03603_rag__chunk_002> a kg:Chunk ;
    kg:chunkId "2003.03603_rag::chunk_002" ;
    kg:docId "2003.03603_rag" ;
    kg:inPaper <http://example.org/kg/paper/2003_03603_rag> ;
    kg:textSpan "follows: We propose a scheme called Generative Low-bitwidth Data Free Quantization (GDFQ), which performs 4-bitwidth quantization without any real data. To our knowledge, this is the rst low-bitwidth data free quantization method. We propose an eective knowledge matching generator to construct data by mining knowledge from the pre-trained full-precision model. The generated data retain classication boundary knowledge and data distribution. Extensive experiments on image classication data sets de" .

<http://example.org/kg/chunk/2003_03603_rag__chunk_006> a kg:Chunk ;
    kg:chunkId "2003.03603_rag::chunk_006" ;
    kg:docId "2003.03603_rag" ;
    kg:inPaper <http://example.org/kg/paper/2003_03603_rag> ;
    kg:textSpan "are able to learn a good generator to keep the distribution information from the training data. 4.2 Fake-data Driven Low-bitwidth Quantization With the help of the generator, the data-free quantization problem can be turned to a supervised quantization problem. Thus, we are able to quantize a model using produced meaningful data. However, transferring knowledge from a pretrained model to a quantized model is dicult. To address this, we introduce a fake-data driven quantization method and solve t" .

<http://example.org/kg/chunk/2003_03603_rag__chunk_007> a kg:Chunk ;
    kg:chunkId "2003.03603_rag::chunk_007" ;
    kg:docId "2003.03603_rag" ;
    kg:inPaper <http://example.org/kg/paper/2003_03603_rag> ;
    kg:textSpan "be trained with the generated data to perform multi-class classication. Second, the traditional ne-tuning process with a common classication loss function is insucient because the data are fake. However, with the help of fake data, we are able to apply knowledge distillation [ 17 ] to improve the quantization performance. Specically, given the same inputs, the outputs of a quantized model and full-precision model should be close enough to guarantee that the quantized model is able to achieve nea" .

