    kg:textSpan "Figure 1. (a) Previous nonuniform quantization function outputs weights and activations in in-equidistant levels, which requires the post-processing of mapping oating-point levels to binary digits in order to obtain the speed-up effect of quantization [ 1 , 13 , 52 ]. (b) The proposed N2UQ learns input thresholds to allow more exibility, while outputs uniformly quantized values, enabling hardwarefriendly linear mappings and efcient bitwise operations. The intractable gradient computation w.r.t. " .

<http://example.org/kg/chunk/2111_14826_rag__chunk_002> a kg:Chunk ;
    kg:chunkId "2111.14826_rag::chunk_002" ;
    kg:docId "2111.14826_rag" ;
    kg:inPaper <http://example.org/kg/paper/2111_14826_rag> ;
    kg:textSpan "weights/activations) to have uniform quantization levels in order not to incur additional postprocessing tasks, each of the output levels does not necessarily need to represent an equal range of the real-valued input. As shown in Fig. 1 (b), we enforce the output quantization levels to be equidistant while learning the thresholds on the input values to incorporate more exibility in tting the underlying real-valued distributions for quantization. We name this quantizer design as Nonuniform-toUnif" .

<http://example.org/kg/chunk/2111_14826_rag__chunk_004> a kg:Chunk ;
    kg:chunkId "2111.14826_rag::chunk_004" ;
    kg:docId "2111.14826_rag" ;
    kg:inPaper <http://example.org/kg/paper/2111_14826_rag> ;
    kg:textSpan "achieve nonuniform quantization. QIL [ 25 ] proposes to learn a power function before the STE-based quantizer for controlling quantization interval sizes. However, these methods relying on STE suffer from the discrepancy between quantizers thresholds and nonlinear functions turning points [ 48 ] or the inexibility of using a human-dened smooth function to control all the quantization intervals with a single power hyper-parameter [ 25 ]. To overcome this limitation, we proposed Generalized Straig" .

<http://example.org/kg/chunk/2111_14826_rag__chunk_007> a kg:Chunk ;
    kg:chunkId "2111.14826_rag::chunk_007" ;
    kg:docId "2111.14826_rag" ;
    kg:inPaper <http://example.org/kg/paper/2111_14826_rag> ;
    kg:textSpan "i,l = To this end, we show that STE encodes the expectation of stochastic binarization in the backward approximation to the forward deterministic binarization functions. Lemma 2 . A quantization function x q can be regarded as a summation of binarization functions x b with different thresholds: x q = x b thre 1 + x b thre 2 + ... + x b thre n . This lemma is self-evident, as illustrated in Fig. 2 (b). From here, we extend the concept of stochastic binarization to quantization for deriving G-STE " .

<http://example.org/kg/chunk/2111_14826_rag__chunk_013> a kg:Chunk ;
    kg:chunkId "2111.14826_rag::chunk_013" ;
    kg:docId "2111.14826_rag" ;
    kg:inPaper <http://example.org/kg/paper/2111_14826_rag> ;
    kg:textSpan "by the extrema. Specically, for the 2-bit case, only 6 entries in the weight lter are quantized to 0 . 5 and 20 entries are quantized to 0 . 5 in the baseline method. In contrast, the proposed entropy-based regularization method produces more evenly distributed quantized weights, which evidently enhances the amount of information that quantized weights carry. In Fig. 3 (b), the classic uniform quantization has xed input thresholds, making it hard to adjust itself to t different underlying distri" .

<http://example.org/kg/chunk/2201_07703_rag__chunk_005> a kg:Chunk ;
    kg:chunkId "2201.07703_rag::chunk_005" ;
    kg:docId "2201.07703_rag" ;
    kg:inPaper <http://example.org/kg/paper/2201_07703_rag> ;
    kg:textSpan "this method. In order to resolve these disadvantages, we introduce head-wise bit-width and switchable scale, as shown in Fig. 3 . Head-wise Bit-width: Unlike CNN, there is a special architecture in ViT: MSA. As we discuss in Sec. 3.2 , the quantization sensitivity varies largely among different heads. Thus, different heads should be allocated with different bitwidths based on their quantization robustness. Inspired by this discovery, we propose head-wise bit-width for ViT. Specically, each head " .

<http://example.org/kg/chunk/2201_07703_rag__chunk_007> a kg:Chunk ;
    kg:chunkId "2201.07703_rag::chunk_007" ;
    kg:docId "2201.07703_rag" ;
    kg:inPaper <http://example.org/kg/paper/2201_07703_rag> ;
    kg:textSpan "also has a complexity constraint. In previous works, the complexity constraint is miscellaneous, such as memory footprints [ 30 ], model size [ 30 ], BitOPs [ 12 , 32 ]. In Q-ViT, we adopt BitOPs as the complexity constraint because it is hardware-agnostic and can be calculated with model denition and bit-width allocation directly. With this constraint, the optimization for Q-ViT under the BitOPs constraint c can be formulated as: = x max x min 2 b (11) min W,S,B E X D [ F ( X, W, S, B )] s.t. C" .

<http://example.org/kg/chunk/2201_07703_rag__chunk_011> a kg:Chunk ;
    kg:chunkId "2201.07703_rag::chunk_011" ;
    kg:docId "2201.07703_rag" ;
    kg:inPaper <http://example.org/kg/paper/2201_07703_rag> ;
    kg:textSpan "middle layers. These observations are consistent with high importance of these layers. 5. Conclusion In this work, we analyze the quantization robustness of all components in the transformer layers. This demonstrates the quantization characteristics of ViT and provides some insights for ViT quantization. Based on this analysis, we propose Q-ViT, a fully differentiable quantization method, which consists two features: head-wise bit-width and switchable scale. To our knowledge, it is the rst work " .

